 "script": "Good morning, Yamuna. It's Thursday the nineteenth of November, and I thought we might begin with a few riddles.\n\nThe Guardian has a lively piece on Jan van Eyck, whose ten surviving portraits look so vividly, almost uncomfortably real. The feature's argument is that they are also full of tricks and traps: cryptic inscriptions, little hidden details, secret messages that the sitters, and we, are invited to puzzle over. It comes with a review of the exhibition Van Eyck: The Portraits. If you like the moment when a painting starts talking back to the written word, this one is for you.\n\nFrom paint to the page, and from the page to a screen. Arte has put online a French-language discussion on Virginia Woolf, called Un lieu à soi, une voix pour toutes. It revisits her 1929 essay A Room of One's Own, with contemporary writers including Marie Darrieussecq and Titiou Lecoq. Since you are teaching literature in French surroundings, it could be a lovely thing to dip into, perhaps to hear how Woolf sounds when she is carried into another language and another generation.\n\nNow, a novel. The Guardian reviews The Hill, a debut by Harriet Clark, and calls it distinctive. Its narrator is a girl growing up with a mother who is serving a life sentence. The prison visits give the book its rhythm, and woven through them are the war-weary memories of the girl's grandmother. That is a young voice and an old one, side by side, which is just the kind of narrator-and-history pairing you tend to enjoy.\n\nStaying with the way history can be told, there is an obituary in the Guardian of the Oxford historian Theodore Zeldin. He was celebrated for a novelistic approach to history, and for his Oxford Muse conversations. It is a short read, but it might send you back to the idea that the past is best understood as a set of human stories, rather than a list of dates.\n\nAnd for the drive, or the walk to school, there is In Our Time on Radio 4, this week on Ishtar, the Mesopotamian goddess of love and war. She was worshipped for more than three and a half thousand years, and the programme describes her as transgressive. It runs to fifty-three minutes, so it is one for a longer journey, and one that rewards you with a very old kind of storytelling.\n\nAnd one good thing before you go. Hannah Lillith Assadi has won the US National Book Award for fiction with Paradiso 17, which the Guardian reports as a visionary novel. It is drawn from her late father's escape from the Nakba, so a story that began in a family has now been honoured as a work of fiction. It is good to see a book rooted in so much memory given such a bright moment.\n\nHave a lovely day, Yamuna, and I hope something beautiful turns up in your classroom.",
 "words": 491,
 "teaser": "Van Eyck's hidden riddles, Woolf in French, and a prize-winning novel",
